            <ArticleTitle>318 Lumbar Disc Surgery: Clinical Outcome of 85 Patients With a Mean Follow-up of 32 Years.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>192</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1227/01.neu.0000489807.67853.ee</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Surgery of lumbar disc herniation is one of the most common neurosurgical procedures. Since the introduction of the microscope, the so-called microscopic discectomy (MD) has represented the &quot;gold standard&quot; technique until today. Here, the authors present clinical outcome of patients treated for of lumbar disc herniation with a mean follow-up of more than 30 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A retrospective outcome study enrolled 85 patients who underwent a 1- to 2-level MD or standard open discectomy (SOD) 27 to 40 years ago. The pre- and postoperative clinical status and the surgical record were analyzed for all patients. A standardized questionnaire which included the current neurological status, EQ-5D, PSI, Odom criteria, Oswestry Disability Index (ODI), and limitations in quality of life because of the operation was answered by each patient.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The mean follow-up was 32.1 years, 50 patients were operated on via MD, and 35 via SOD. Preoperatively, the mean pain intensity for leg pain was 9 on the Numeric Pain Rating Scale (NPRS), and 1 at follow-up. Median EQ-5D was 6, according to PSI 93% of the patients were satisfied, according to Odom criteria 91% of the patients reported good-to-excellent functional recovery. The mean ODI was 15% (range 0-60%). Eight patients had a second surgery because of recurrent disc herniation, 13 patients had a second surgery because of a new disc herniation at the adjacent level. Two patients underwent a third procedure. The recurrent herniation rate was 8% in the MD group and 12% in SOD group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The long-term follow-up data of this study reveals excellent clinical results even after more than 30 years. Overall rate for second surgery was 25%. About 15% of all patients needed a second surgery because of a disc herniation at the adjacent segment. No difference in long-term clinical outcome could be demonstrated for microdiscectomy and open discectomy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
